findings.description:
The "increment per run" rule copies the tech-debt-audit series, where "each run is an independent sample." An overview is not a sample. It restates the current plan and status, so each regeneration replaces the last one. Giving it a new index each time leads to:
- several `94x-analysis.overview.md` files, with the older ones holding out-of-date status;
- no marker saying which one supersedes which;
- the "Ten slots… not enough" pressure the doc then uses to justify overflow.

Give the overview one stable file per project (or per topic) and overwrite it. git history already keeps the old versions. The same question applies to `codebase-comprehension`: once the graph is refreshed, is a re-run a new sample or a replacement? Decide explicitly. This removes the need for the overflow rule altogether.

The contract depends on exact field names (`tour[]`, `layers[].nodeIds`, `complexity`, type-prefixed node IDs) and on upstream phase numbers ("Phase 6", "Phase 7", "Phase 0"). The doc itself calls the plugin "actively developed (v2.8.1)."

Nothing covers:
- recording or checking the plugin or graph version, for example from `meta.json`;
- what happens when an expected key is missing or renamed;
- the case where `tour[]` is absent or empty. A partial or failed `/understand` run, or a future version that makes the tour optional, would leave the Solution Approach section with no source.

Without these, a skill that reads "the needed section" quietly gets nothing and drafts from whatever is left. That breaks the fail-explicitly rule. Define a minimum required shape and a hard stop with a message when it isn't met.
